                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Sigh...\n\nI get what this game is trying to do. Being a refugee sucks. I get it.\n\nBut this story is told in the most unemotional, flat way possible. There's no passion here. Oh sure it exists in words, but the feelings never pop off the page. The writing needed to be much more compelling to pull this off.\n\nThe gameplay is purposefully repetitive, I get it. But the way the game messes with you just doesn't make logical sense. I figured it out eventually, but there's so much more that could've made it better. I get that the system being hard to understand and throwing random obstacles in your way could be interpreted as a larger metaphor, and in another game I'd be willing to concede that point, but it simply doesn't work here. Once you find out how to game the numbers it's trivial to blow through to the end without any problems.\n\nThe single biggest misstep is that they could have allowed for choices and branching paths leading to some interesting conclusions. They built in the mechanics! It's right there! But instead of following through, in the most frustrating game design choice possible, the game enforces its own moral code on you, and in the few places where you can make a morally gray decision it just gives you a game over. You made the wrong choice. Now reload and make the one we want you to.\n\nIt's not a bad concept, but the execution leaves much to be desired. Go play Always Sometimes Monsters for a better experience in the same vein.",
